## Project Overview

Mem-Forge-OS is a single-CPU-capable, 32-bit x86, Unix-like teaching operating system derived from xv6. Its distinguishing feature is a hand-written Copy-on-Write implementation that touches the process-creation path (`fork`), the virtual-memory subsystem (page tables, page-fault handling), and the physical page allocator (reference counting).

In the stock xv6 kernel, `fork()` calls `copyuvm()`, which walks every page-table entry of the parent up to the process size, allocates a brand-new physical page for each one, and `memmove()`s the contents across. This is correct but wasteful: most of a freshly forked child's memory is never modified before an `exec()` replaces it (the classic `fork()`+`exec()` shell pattern), so the eager copy does real work that is thrown away almost immediately.

This repository keeps `copyuvm()` in `kernel/vm.c` untouched but **no longer calls it from `fork()`**. Instead, `fork()` calls a new function, `cowuvm()`, which:

- Builds a new page directory for the child.
- Reuses the parent's existing physical pages instead of copying them.
- Clears the writable bit (`PTE_W`) on the mapping in **both** the parent and the child.
- Increments a per-physical-page reference count for every page it shares.

The actual copy only happens later, inside the page-fault handler, and only for the specific 4 KB page that was written to — not the whole address space.

## Problem Statement

**Traditional `fork()`:**

```
Parent memory
      │
      ▼
Copy entire address space (every page, every byte)
      │
      ▼
Child memory (fully independent, fully duplicated)
```

Every call to `fork()` in stock xv6 pays for:

- One `kalloc()` per page of the parent's address space.
- One `memmove()` of `PGSIZE` bytes per page.
- One `mappages()` call per page to install the new mapping.

This is proportional to the **entire size of the parent process**, regardless of how much of that memory the child will ever touch. For workloads that immediately call `exec()` after `fork()` (the dominant pattern for shells and most Unix tooling — see `user/sh.c` in this repository), nearly all of that copied memory is discarded before it is ever read or written.

**Copy-on-Write, as implemented here:**

```
Parent ──────┐
             ├── Shared physical pages (both mappings marked read-only)
Child  ──────┘
             │
             ▼
   A write by either process triggers a page fault
             │
             ▼
   Kernel allocates ONE new private page, copies ONE page of data,
   remaps only the faulting virtual address
```

`fork()` becomes an O(page-table-size) pointer-sharing operation instead of an O(address-space-size) copy, and the cost of actually duplicating data is deferred — and often avoided entirely — until a write makes it unavoidable.

## Core Innovation

Compared with the stock xv6 behavior, this repository changes:

| Mechanism | Stock xv6 | Mem-Forge-OS |
|---|---|---|
| Page sharing on fork | None — every page is copied immediately | Parent and child map the **same physical page** immediately after fork |
| Page permissions | Child pages get parent's original flags (writable if parent's were) | `cowuvm()` clears `PTE_W` on the shared mapping in **both** processes |
| Write protection | N/A | A cleared `PTE_W` bit on a `PTE_P` page is the CoW signal used by the fault handler |
| Page faults | Unhandled CoW case; any page fault falls through to killing the process | `trap()` calls `cowpgflthandler()` on `T_PGFLT` before falling back to the kill path |
| Private page creation | N/A (already private) | `cowpgflthandler()` allocates a new page, copies the old page's contents, remaps only the faulting page |
| Reference management | N/A | `kmem.ref_cnt[]`, a per-physical-page array in `kernel/kalloc.c`, tracked via `kincrement()` / `kdecrement()` |
| Cleanup | `kfree()` called unconditionally on process teardown | `kdecrement()` called instead everywhere a page might still be shared (`freevm`, `deallocuvm`, the fault handler) |

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[User Programs<br/>sh, ls, cat, test_1, test_3, forktest, usertests] --> B[System Call Interface<br/>kernel/syscall.c, kernel/sysproc.c]
    B --> C[Process Management<br/>kernel/proc.c: fork, exit, wait, scheduler]
    C --> D[Virtual Memory / Page Tables<br/>kernel/vm.c: walkpgdir, mappages, cowuvm]
    D --> E[Copy-on-Write Mechanism<br/>kernel/vm.c: cowpgflthandler]
    E --> F[Physical Memory Allocator<br/>kernel/kalloc.c: kalloc, kfree, ref_cnt array]
    F --> G[x86 Hardware / QEMU<br/>32-bit protected mode, hardware page tables, MMU]
    H[Trap / Interrupt Layer<br/>kernel/trap.c, kernel/trapasm.S] -.T_PGFLT.-> E
    C -.syscall dispatch.-> H
```

- **User Programs** — the xv6 shell and utilities in `user/`, plus the CoW-specific test programs `test_1.c` and `test_3.c`.
- **System Call Interface** — `kernel/syscall.c` dispatches to handlers in `kernel/sysproc.c` / `kernel/sysfile.c`; `SYS_fork` and the custom `SYS_getFreePagesCount` are both routed through this layer.
- **Process Management** — `kernel/proc.c` owns the process table, `fork()`, `exit()`, `wait()`, and the scheduler.
- **Virtual Memory / Page Tables** — `kernel/vm.c` owns page-directory/page-table manipulation (`walkpgdir`, `mappages`) and the CoW setup routine `cowuvm()`.
- **Copy-on-Write Mechanism** — `cowpgflthandler()` in `kernel/vm.c`, invoked from the trap handler.
- **Physical Memory Allocator** — `kernel/kalloc.c`, extended with a reference-count array (`kmem.ref_cnt[]`) sized `PHYSTOP / PGSIZE`.
- **Trap / Interrupt Layer** — `kernel/trap.c` and `kernel/trapasm.S` route the x86 `#PF` (vector 14, `T_PGFLT`) exception into the CoW handler before falling back to xv6's default "kill the process" behavior.
- **x86 Hardware / QEMU** — the kernel targets 32-bit protected mode (`-m32`, `--omagic`, no PIC) and is booted from a raw disk image inside QEMU.

## Copy-on-Write Architecture

### Phase 1 — `fork()`

`kernel/proc.c: fork()` allocates a new `struct proc` via `allocproc()`, then — instead of the stock `copyuvm()` call — invokes `cowuvm(proc->pgdir, proc->sz)` to build the child's address space.

### Phase 2 — Address Space Sharing

`cowuvm()` (`kernel/vm.c`) walks the parent's page table one `PGSIZE` entry at a time. For each present page it:
1. Clears `PTE_W` on the **parent's own** page-table entry and flushes the TLB (`lcr3`).
2. Reads the physical address and flags out of the now-read-only PTE.
3. Calls `mappages()` to install an identical mapping (same physical address, same — now read-only — flags) into the child's new page directory.
4. Calls `kincrement()` on that physical page.

No new physical memory is allocated in this phase; both processes now point at the same physical page, and neither can write to it.

### Phase 3 — Write Protection

Because both the parent's and the child's page-table entries have `PTE_W` cleared, any subsequent store instruction to that page — by either process — is trapped by the x86 MMU as a page-protection fault, entering the kernel at vector `T_PGFLT` (14).

### Phase 4 — Page Fault

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant CPU as x86 CPU (MMU)
    participant Trap as trap() [kernel/trap.c]
    participant CoW as cowpgflthandler() [kernel/vm.c]
    participant Alloc as kalloc()/kmem.ref_cnt [kernel/kalloc.c]

    CPU->>Trap: #PF exception (T_PGFLT), CR2 = faulting VA
    Trap->>CoW: cowpgflthandler(proc)
    CoW->>CoW: walkpgdir() to find the PTE for CR2
    CoW->>CoW: Validate PTE_P set, PTE_W clear (else: not a CoW fault)
    CoW->>Alloc: kgetrefcnt(pa)
    alt ref_cnt > 1 (page still shared)
        CoW->>Alloc: kalloc() a new physical page
        CoW->>CoW: memmove() old page contents into new page
        CoW->>CoW: mappages() the faulting VA to the new page, PTE_W set
        CoW->>Alloc: kdecrement(old pa)
        CoW->>CPU: lcr3() to flush TLB
    else ref_cnt == 1 (last owner)
        CoW->>CoW: set PTE_W on the existing PTE (no copy needed)
        CoW->>CPU: lcr3() to flush TLB
    end
    CoW->>Trap: return 1 (handled)
    Trap->>CPU: lapiceoi(), resume faulting instruction
```

If `cowpgflthandler()` returns `0` (not a recognized CoW fault — e.g. the PTE was already writable, not present, or invalid), `kernel/trap.c` falls through to xv6's default trap path, which prints diagnostics and sets `proc->killed = 1`.

## Memory Model

| Concept | Meaning in this repository |
|---|---|
| Virtual address | The address a user (or kernel) instruction uses; translated through the process's page directory/table. |
| Physical address | The real address in RAM, as tracked by `kernel/kalloc.c`. |
| Page | A 4096-byte (`PGSIZE`) unit of memory, the granularity at which xv6 (and this fork) manages both virtual mappings and physical allocation. |
| Page table / page directory | The two-level x86 structures walked by `walkpgdir()` in `kernel/vm.c`; each process has its own `pgdir`. |
| Page table entry (PTE) flags | `PTE_P` (present), `PTE_W` (writable), `PTE_U` (user-accessible), defined in `kernel/mmu.h`. CoW uses `PTE_P` + `PTE_W` state, not a dedicated CoW bit — there is no separate `PTE_COW` flag in this implementation. A present-but-not-writable user page is treated as a CoW candidate by `cowpgflthandler()`. |
| Physical page ownership / reference counting | Implemented. `kernel/kalloc.c` adds `uint ref_cnt[PHYSTOP / PGSIZE]` inside the `kmem` struct, indexed by `physical_address / PGSIZE`. `kalloc()` initializes a fresh page's count to 1; `kincrement()`/`kdecrement()` adjust it; `kdecrement()` calls `kfree()` once the count reaches 1 (i.e. the caller is the last owner). |

Physical memory flow in this project: a page starts with `ref_cnt == 1` when first allocated by `kalloc()`. Every `fork()` that shares it bumps the count by one via `kincrement()`. Every teardown path (`freevm`, `deallocuvm`) or CoW copy-on-write event calls `kdecrement()` instead of `kfree()` directly, so a page is only actually returned to the free list once every process (and, in the fault handler, every code path that still references the old page) has released its claim.

## Detailed CoW Execution Flow

```text
fork()                                          [kernel/proc.c]
  ↓
allocproc() — allocate PCB + kernel stack       [kernel/proc.c]
  ↓
cowuvm(parent->pgdir, parent->sz)               [kernel/vm.c]
  ↓
for each mapped page: clear PTE_W in parent,
map same physical page into child (read-only),
kincrement(pa)                                  [kernel/vm.c, kernel/kalloc.c]
  ↓
copy trap frame, clear child's %eax → 0,
dup open files/cwd                              [kernel/proc.c]
  ↓
child marked RUNNABLE, scheduled normally        [kernel/proc.c]
  ↓
child (or parent) writes to a shared page
  ↓
x86 MMU raises #PF (T_PGFLT)
  ↓
trap() dispatches to cowpgflthandler()          [kernel/trap.c → kernel/vm.c]
  ↓
walkpgdir() locates the PTE; validate P set / W clear
  ↓
kgetrefcnt(pa) — check current reference count  [kernel/kalloc.c]
  ↓
ref_cnt > 1: kalloc() new page, memmove() old→new,
             mappages() faulting VA → new page (W=1),
             kdecrement(old pa)
ref_cnt == 1: just set PTE_W on the existing PTE (no copy needed —
             this process is already the sole owner)
  ↓
lcr3() flush TLB, resume faulting instruction
```

## Source Code Architecture

```text
Mem-Forge-OS/
├── include/          # Headers shared between kernel and user space
├── kernel/           # Kernel source: boot, VM, process mgmt, drivers, traps
├── user/             # User-space programs, shell, libc-lite, tests
├── tools/            # Host-side build tools (mkfs) and QEMU/GDB helpers
├── Makefile           # Top-level build orchestration
├── FILES              # One-line description of each top-level directory
└── README.md
```

### `kernel/`

| File | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `proc.c` / `proc.h` | Process table, `fork()`, `exit()`, `wait()`, scheduler, context switching entry points. |
| `vm.c` | Page-directory/page-table management: `setupkvm`, `walkpgdir`, `mappages`, `allocuvm`, `deallocuvm`, `freevm`, legacy `copyuvm`, and the CoW-specific `cowuvm` / `cowpgflthandler`. |
| `kalloc.c` | Physical page allocator; extended in this repository with the `ref_cnt[]` array and `kincrement`/`kdecrement`/`kgetrefcnt`, plus the `sys_getFreePagesCount` support function. |
| `trap.c` / `trapasm.S` | Trap/interrupt dispatch; `trap.c` routes `T_PGFLT` into `cowpgflthandler()` before the default fault-handling path. |
| `sysproc.c` | Process-related syscall bodies (`sys_fork`, `sys_exit`, `sys_wait`, etc. — see `kernel/sysfunc.h` for the full mapping). |
| `syscall.c` | Syscall number → handler dispatch table, including the added `SYS_getFreePagesCount` entry. |
| `exec.c` | `execve()`-style program loading; calls `freevm()` on the old address space after a successful exec. |
| `mmu.h` | x86 MMU structure/flag definitions (`PTE_P`, `PTE_W`, `PTE_U`, segment descriptors, etc.). |
| `main.c`, `bootasm.S`, `bootmain.c`, `multiboot.S`, `bootother.S`, `data.S`, `initcode.S` | Boot sequence, multiboot header, AP bootstrap, and the first user program stub. |
| `console.c`, `uart.c`, `kbd.c`, `ide.c`, `lapic.c`, `ioapic.c`, `picirq.c`, `mp.c`, `timer.c` | Device drivers and multiprocessor/interrupt-controller support (unmodified xv6 subsystems, not part of the CoW path). |
| `fs.c`, `bio.c`, `file.c`, `pipe.c`, `sysfile.c` | Filesystem, buffer cache, file-descriptor, and pipe support (unmodified xv6 subsystems). |
| `spinlock.c`, `string.c`, `swtch.S` | Locking primitives, libc-style string helpers, and the low-level context-switch routine. |

### `user/`

- `sh.c` — the xv6 shell; the primary real-world driver of `fork()`+`exec()`.
- `init.c` — PID-1 equivalent, spawns the shell.
- Standard utilities: `cat.c`, `echo.c`, `grep.c`, `kill.c`, `ln.c`, `ls.c`, `mkdir.c`, `rm.c`, `wc.c`, `zombie.c`, `stressfs.c`.
- `ulib.c`, `umalloc.c`, `printf.c`, `usys.S`, `user.h` — the minimal user-space library (string/memory helpers, a simple `malloc`/`free`, syscall stubs, and syscall prototypes including the added `getFreePagesCount()`).
- **CoW-relevant tests:** `test_1.c`, `test_3.c`, `forktest.c`, `usertests.c` — see [Running Tests](#running-tests).
- `tester.c` — an explicit grading-script placeholder (its header comment says not to modify it).

### `include/`

Headers shared between kernel and user code: `types.h`, `stat.h`, `fcntl.h`, `fs.h`, `param.h`, `syscall.h` (syscall numbers, including `SYS_getFreePagesCount`), `traps.h` (trap vector numbers, including `T_PGFLT`), `x86.h`.

### `tools/`

- `mkfs.c` — host-side tool that builds the `fs.img` filesystem image from the compiled user binaries.
- `dot-gdbinit` — template `.gdbinit`, wired up by the Makefile with the correct QEMU GDB stub port.
- `gdbutil` — GDB helper macros for pretty-printing x86 segment/gate descriptors.
- `dot-bochsrc` — configuration template for running under Bochs as an alternative to QEMU (`make bochs`).
- `makefile.mk` — build rules for `tools/mkfs`.

## Key Files Changed for CoW

| File | Responsibility | CoW Relevance |
|---|---|---|
| `kernel/proc.c` | Process creation (`fork()`) | `fork()` calls `cowuvm()` instead of the stock `copyuvm()` to build the child's address space. |
| `kernel/vm.c` | Virtual memory / page tables | Adds `cowuvm()` (shares pages read-only, increments ref counts) and `cowpgflthandler()` (resolves write faults on shared pages). Also updates `freevm()` and `deallocuvm()` to call `kdecrement()` instead of `kfree()` directly, so shared pages aren't freed while still referenced. The original eager-copy `copyuvm()` is left intact but is no longer called by `fork()`. |
| `kernel/kalloc.c` | Physical page allocator | Adds the `ref_cnt[]` reference-count array, `kincrement()`, `kdecrement()`, `kgetrefcnt()`, and `sys_getFreePagesCount()` (free-page instrumentation used by the tests). |
| `kernel/trap.c` | Trap/interrupt dispatch | Routes `T_PGFLT` (page fault) into `cowpgflthandler()` before falling back to the default "kill the process" trap path. |
| `kernel/defs.h` | Kernel-wide function prototypes | Declares `cowuvm`, `kincrement`, `kdecrement` (and retains `copyuvm`) so they're visible across translation units. |
| `kernel/sysfunc.h` / `include/syscall.h` / `kernel/syscall.c` | Syscall plumbing | Wire up `SYS_getFreePagesCount` (syscall number 22) so user programs can query the current free-page count for verification/testing. |
| `user/user.h`, `user/usys.S` | User-space syscall stubs | Expose `getFreePagesCount()` to user programs (used by `test_1.c` / `test_3.c`). |
| `user/test_1.c`, `user/test_3.c` | CoW verification | New test programs written for this project (see [Running Tests](#running-tests)). |

## Build System

The build is orchestrated by the top-level `Makefile`, which includes three sub-makefiles:

- `kernel/makefile.mk` — builds `kernel/kernel` and the bootable `xv6.img`.
- `user/makefile.mk` — builds every program listed in `USER_PROGS` (`cat`, `echo`, `forktest`, `grep`, `init`, `kill`, `ln`, `ls`, `mkdir`, `rm`, `sh`, `stressfs`, `tester`, `usertests`, `wc`, `zombie`, `test_1`, `test_3`).
- `tools/makefile.mk` — builds the host-side `tools/mkfs` utility used to assemble `fs.img`.

Toolchain, as declared in the Makefile:

- **Compiler:** `gcc` (`CC := gcc`)
- **Assembler:** `gcc` acting as assembler front-end (`AS := gcc`)
- **Linker:** `ld` (`LD := ld`), with `OBJCOPY` / `OBJDUMP` from binutils
- **Target:** 32-bit x86, via `-m32` on both kernel and user builds
- **Kernel flags:** `-nostdinc -fno-pic -fno-builtin -fno-strict-aliasing -fno-stack-protector -m32`, linked with `-nostdlib --omagic` and an explicit `--section-start=.text=0x100000 --entry=main`
- **User flags:** the same `-m32 -fno-pic -fno-builtin -fno-strict-aliasing -fno-stack-protector`, linked against the minimal `user/ulib.o usys.o printf.o umalloc.o`

Running `make` (the default target, `all: xv6.img fs.img`) does the following:

1. Compiles every kernel object listed in `KERNEL_OBJECTS` and links them, along with `bootother` and `initcode` binary blobs, into `kernel/kernel`.
2. Assembles and links the boot sector (`kernel/bootblock`) from `bootasm.S` + `bootmain.c`, signed via `kernel/sign.pl`.
3. Concatenates the boot sector and kernel image into the bootable disk image `xv6.img`.
4. Compiles every program in `USER_PROGS` against the minimal user library.
5. Runs `tools/mkfs` to pack the compiled user binaries (plus `README.md`, copied in as `fs/README`) into the `fs.img` filesystem image.

Verified Make targets (present in the `Makefile`'s `.PHONY` declaration and recipes):

```bash
make             # build xv6.img and fs.img (default: 'all')
make clean       # remove all build artifacts
make run         # alias for 'make qemu'
make qemu        # boot in QEMU with a graphical console
make qemu-nox    # boot in QEMU, serial console only (no graphics)
make qemu-gdb    # boot in QEMU, paused, waiting for a GDB connection (graphical)
make qemu-nox-gdb# boot in QEMU, paused, waiting for a GDB connection (serial only)
make bochs       # boot under the Bochs emulator instead of QEMU
make depend      # (re)generate .d dependency files
make dist        # package a source tarball via 'git describe'
```

> **QEMU binary name.** The Makefile auto-detects an executable literally named `qemu` on your `PATH` (falling back to a UW-Madison CS 537 lab path that will not exist outside that course). Modern QEMU packages typically install `qemu-system-i386` instead of a bare `qemu` binary. If `make qemu`/`make qemu-nox` reports it cannot find QEMU, override the variable explicitly, e.g. `make QEMU=qemu-system-i386 qemu-nox`. This is inferred directly from the Makefile's QEMU-detection logic, not a claim about what is installed on any particular system.

## Windows Installation Guide (WSL2)

Because the kernel targets 32-bit x86 with `-nostdlib`/`-nostdinc` and produces a raw disk image booted directly by QEMU, the most reliable way to build and run Mem-Forge-OS on Windows is **WSL2 with an Ubuntu distribution**, not a native Windows toolchain.

```
Windows
   │
   ▼
WSL2
   │
   ▼
Ubuntu (userspace)
   │
   ▼
gcc / make / qemu-system-i386
   │
   ▼
Mem-Forge-OS (xv6.img + fs.img booted in QEMU)
```

### Step 1 — Install WSL2

Open **PowerShell as Administrator** and run:

```powershell
wsl --install
```

This installs WSL2 and a default Ubuntu distribution, and requires a restart on first install.

### Step 2 — Install Ubuntu (if not installed automatically)

```powershell
wsl --install -d Ubuntu
```

Launch it from the Start menu ("Ubuntu") and complete the first-run prompts to create a Unix username and password.

### Step 3 — Update Ubuntu packages

Inside the Ubuntu terminal:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
```

### Step 4 — Install required dependencies

The kernel needs a 32-bit-capable GCC toolchain, GNU Make, Perl (used by `kernel/vectors.pl` and `kernel/sign.pl`), QEMU, and GDB:

```bash
sudo apt install -y build-essential gcc-multilib g++-multilib \
    make perl gdb qemu-system-x86
```

`gcc-multilib`/`g++-multilib` provide the 32-bit (`-m32`) support that a default 64-bit Ubuntu GCC install does not include on its own.

### Step 5 — Verify the toolchain

```bash
gcc --version
make --version
qemu-system-i386 --version
gdb --version
```

All four commands should print version information without error.

### Step 6 — Clone the repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/noohkhan7232/Mem-Forge-OS.git
cd Mem-Forge-OS
```

### Step 7 — Build

```bash
make
```

This produces `xv6.img` and `fs.img` in the repository root.

### Step 8 — Run

```bash
make QEMU=qemu-system-i386 qemu-nox
```

(`qemu-nox` is recommended under WSL since it avoids needing a graphical display server; see the note in [Build System](#build-system) about why `QEMU=qemu-system-i386` is passed explicitly.)

### Step 9 — Exit QEMU

- In `qemu-nox` (serial-only) mode: press **Ctrl-a**, release, then press **x** to quit QEMU (this is the standard QEMU monitor escape sequence; the Makefile prints "Ctrl+a h for help" on startup, which lists all available escape commands including quitting).
- In graphical `qemu` mode: close the QEMU window, or use the same **Ctrl-a x** sequence in the associated console.

### Step 10 — Clean build artifacts

```bash
make clean
```

## Native Windows Option

**Not verified in the repository.** The Makefile assumes a POSIX shell, GNU Make, a GNU/Linux-targeting `gcc`/`ld`/`objcopy`/`objdump` toolchain, and Perl scripts invoked directly (`kernel/vectors.pl`, `kernel/sign.pl`), none of which are native to Windows without a POSIX layer such as WSL2, Cygwin, or MSYS2. There is no MSVC project, no native Windows build script, and no evidence in the repository that a native (non-WSL) Windows build has been tested. WSL2 is the recommended path specifically because it provides the Linux userland (bash, GNU Make, Perl, an ELF-targeting GCC) that the build system was written for, without requiring any changes to the Makefile.

## Linux Installation

On Ubuntu/Debian-family systems:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y build-essential gcc-multilib g++-multilib make perl gdb qemu-system-x86

git clone https://github.com/noohkhan7232/Mem-Forge-OS.git
cd Mem-Forge-OS

make
make QEMU=qemu-system-i386 qemu-nox   # or 'qemu' for a graphical console
```

Exit with **Ctrl-a x** (see [Step 9](#step-9--exit-qemu) above). Clean with `make clean`.

If your distribution's `qemu` package already installs a binary literally named `qemu`, the plain `make qemu` / `make qemu-nox` invocations will work without the `QEMU=` override; this varies by distribution and package version.

## macOS

**Not verified in the repository.** The build system is written against a GNU toolchain (`gcc`, `ld`/`objcopy`/`objdump` from GNU binutils) and a Linux-style ELF pipeline. macOS ships Clang under the `gcc` name and a different linker by default, and does not include GNU binutils' `objcopy`/`objdump` out of the box. It is plausible that a working setup could be assembled via Homebrew (`brew install i686-elf-binutils qemu`, cross-compiler toolchains, etc.), but no such configuration is present in, or verified against, this repository. Treat any macOS setup as experimental and expect to adapt the Makefile's `CC`/`AS`/`LD`/`OBJCOPY`/`OBJDUMP` variables to point at a genuine i386-ELF cross-toolchain.

## Running the Operating System

```bash
make
make QEMU=qemu-system-i386 qemu-nox
```

(Substitute `qemu` for `qemu-nox` if you want the graphical console; drop the `QEMU=` override if your system provides a bare `qemu` binary — see [Build System](#build-system).)

On boot you should see the standard xv6 boot sequence (CPU initialization messages, then the `init: starting sh` message from `user/init.c`), followed by the xv6 shell prompt (`$`) served by `user/sh.c`. From the shell you can run any of the compiled user programs by name — e.g. `ls`, `cat README`, `echo hello`, `test_1`, `test_3`, `forktest`, `usertests` — each of which is packaged into `fs.img` by `tools/mkfs` at build time. Every one of these invocations from the shell itself exercises `fork()` (to spawn the child that then `exec()`s the requested program), so simply using the shell is already running the Copy-on-Write path described in this document.

Shut down by exiting QEMU with **Ctrl-a x** (or closing the QEMU window in graphical mode); xv6 itself has no shutdown/halt command exposed at the shell.

